Classic read-modify-write race. Two requests read same value before either writes back.

**Fix: atomic `UPDATE ... RETURNING`**

```sql
-- BAD: two round trips, race window between them
SELECT counter FROM table WHERE id = $1;
-- ... compute new value in app ...
UPDATE table SET counter = $2 WHERE id = $1;

-- GOOD: single atomic operation
UPDATE table SET counter = counter + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING counter;
```

**In Node.js:**

```js
// Bad
const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT counter FROM items WHERE id = $1', [id]);
const newVal = rows[0].counter + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE items SET counter = $2 WHERE id = $1', [id, newVal]);
res.json({ counter: newVal }); // stale under concurrency

// Good
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'UPDATE items SET counter = counter + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING counter',
  [id]
);
res.json({ counter: rows[0].counter }); // always correct
```

**If you need conditional logic around the increment**, use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` to lock the row:

```js
await db.query('BEGIN');
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'SELECT counter FROM items WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE',
  [id]
);
// safe to read + conditionally update — row locked until COMMIT
await db.query('UPDATE items SET counter = $2 WHERE id = $1', [id, rows[0].counter + 1]);
await db.query('COMMIT');
```

`FOR UPDATE` blocks other transactions on same row. Use only when condition requires reading first — otherwise plain atomic UPDATE is faster.
